  title: "Precision and Numerical Tradeoffs — Why Fewer Bits Can Make AI Faster",
  date: "2026-09-14",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 034: Chiplets split a large processor into smaller dies, which can improve manufacturing but makes packaging and communication harder.

Lesson 035: Testing removes bad parts before and after assembly, because one failed component can ruin an expensive accelerator package.

Lesson 036: Adding accelerators helps only when memory, packaging, deployment and software can also support more useful work.

Lesson 037: A GPU runs many matrix calculations in parallel, but those math units produce value only when data reaches them fast enough.

Lesson 038: Caches and local storage let a chip reuse data near its math units, reducing slow trips to HBM.

The big idea

Fewer bits can make numbers cheaper to store, move and calculate, but they also increase rounding and overflow risk.

The practical answer is usually mixed precision: use small formats where the model tolerates them and wider formats where errors could damage the result.

Where we are

model values → numerical format → HBM and cache traffic → matrix units → model output

1. What a numerical format represents

AI systems store weights, activations and gradients as binary numbers. Floating-point formats divide their bits among a sign, an exponent and a fraction.

The exponent sets range; the fraction sets how finely nearby values can be distinguished.

FP32 uses 32 bits and provides broad range and fine detail. FP16 uses half as many bits, with less range and precision.

BF16 also uses 16 bits. It keeps FP32's exponent width, giving similar range, but leaves fewer fraction bits for detail. That can help training values that vary greatly in magnitude.

FP8 compresses further, with layouts that trade range against precision. INT8 stores one of 256 integer codes, normally with a scale that maps each code to an approximate real value. Choosing that scale is part of quantization.

2. Why fewer bits can be faster

Suppose an illustrative model has 10 billion parameters.

At 32 bits, or 4 bytes per parameter, the weights occupy about 40 gigabytes. At 16 bits they occupy about 20 gigabytes. At 8 bits they occupy about 10 gigabytes.

Moving a 16-bit value from HBM requires half the bytes of a 32-bit value, so more values fit in each cache, transfer and network message.

Hardware can also perform more supported low-precision multiplications in parallel. Lesson 038's reuse reduces how often data moves; lower precision reduces the bytes moved each time.

3. Why smaller numbers can fail

Conversion rounds values. Tiny values may become zero, large values may overflow, and repeated additions can lose small contributions.

Training is especially sensitive because gradients can be very small and weight updates accumulate over many steps. FP16 training may use loss scaling: temporarily multiply gradients so important small values do not vanish, then undo the scale before updating weights.

Another protection is wider accumulation: multiply lower-precision inputs but add products into an FP32 total. Important state can remain in FP32 while most tensors use BF16 or FP16.

Inference does not update weights, so it can often use more aggressive quantization. Yet some layers, outlier values or tasks requiring fine numerical distinctions may lose quality.

4. Precision is a system choice

Lower precision helps only when hardware, software and the model agree on the format.

Without fast hardware support, format conversions add work. Communication, CPU overhead or unsupported operations may erase a faster matrix multiplication.

